We're shooting various handgun rounds, a 40, a 9, and a 45.

25
00:02:04,181 --> 00:02:09,980
The department uses these rounds, and we get numerous requests when they change ammo to

26
00:02:09,981 --> 00:02:15,220
shoot these rounds at the vest that they're wearing.

27
00:02:15,221 --> 00:02:19,660
Soft armor vests are made of several layers of ultra-dense polycarbon fabrics of different

28
00:02:19,661 --> 00:02:28,980
weaves and thicknesses, cut with laser precision, and combined to make an impenetrable barrier.

29
00:02:28,981 --> 00:02:36,220
Scientific testing helps determine the best design and mix of fabrics.

30
00:02:36,221 --> 00:02:40,280
Assistant technician Ernie Acuna carefully weighs each bullet that will be fired in the

31
00:02:40,281 --> 00:02:45,780
test and loads each casing with a measured charge.

32
00:02:45,781 --> 00:02:50,020
The test is recorded by Chief Lab Technician Rick Gonzalez.

33
00:02:50,021 --> 00:02:51,060
Here's our piece of armor.

34
00:02:51,061 --> 00:02:54,080
Here's the ballistic clay we use, our target indicator.

35
00:02:54,081 --> 00:02:57,880
Here's our high-speed camera that we use, our Doppler radar.

36
00:02:57,881 --> 00:02:59,740
This here is our conograph, where we pick up

37
00:02:59,741 --> 00:03:01,780
the speed of the bullet in feet per second.

38
00:03:01,781 --> 00:03:05,160
And now we're ready to fire.

39
00:03:05,161 --> 00:03:07,540
OK, this will be a fire in a hole.

40
00:03:07,541 --> 00:03:09,020
Ready?

41
00:03:09,021 --> 00:03:09,740
Fire in a hole!

42
00:03:09,741 --> 00:03:10,740
Oh!

43
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:17,240
The lab's high-speed replay reveals the secret

44
00:03:17,241 --> 00:03:18,740
of the vest's stopping power.

45
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:22,140
Here we have the distinctive cross pattern

46
00:03:22,141 --> 00:03:24,740
of the fiber actually engaging the bullet here.

47
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:27,740
What's really fascinating, we can already see

48
00:03:27,741 --> 00:03:31,040
the bullet flattened out and the energy transfer take place.

49
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:35,440
Vests that are bulletproof, or as the pros prefer,

50
00:03:35,441 --> 00:03:38,640
bullet-resistant, use the principle of strength in numbers,

51
00:03:38,641 --> 00:03:41,440
numbers of strong individual threads, and numbers of layers.

52
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:46,300
When a bullet strikes the vest, it tries to push the fibers apart,

53
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:51,060
but the tough strands hold their positions, forcing the bullet to stretch them.

54
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:53,760
Some of the fibers break under the strain,

55
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:59,860
but they are backed up by many more that absorb the energy like a tightly woven net and stop the bullet.

56
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:03,100
Depending on the weave and the types of materials,

57
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:08,440
what the armor actually does is catch the bullet in a successful stop.

58
00:04:08,441 --> 00:04:12,840
It's just like a catcher's mitt, you know, catching a fastball.

59
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:18,200
A look at the target vest reveals that the .45 caliber bullet

60
00:04:18,201 --> 00:04:22,280
penetrated only three layers before being stopped by the net of tough fibers.

61
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:23,120
43.

62
00:04:23,121 --> 00:04:25,900
A police officer wearing the vest would be protected.

63
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:33,680
Experiments involving higher-powered battlefield weapons

64
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:36,960
are conducted at the U.S. Army's Aberdeen Testing Center in Maryland.

65
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:45,440
Here, experts prepare to test an armor critical to the war in Iraq.

66
00:04:45,441 --> 00:04:52,940
What we have in here right now is a Humvee door that you might recognize from any news

67
00:04:52,941 --> 00:04:54,440
broadcast you've seen.

68
00:04:54,441 --> 00:04:56,440
Note the laser.

69
00:04:56,441 --> 00:04:58,440
That'll be our point of impact.

70
00:04:58,441 --> 00:05:01,440
We're gonna button up and then we're gonna take a shot.

71
00:05:01,441 --> 00:05:06,440
This is the weapon system we use at Aberdeen Test Center, testing armor and armor equipment.

72
00:05:06,441 --> 00:05:10,440
We have a universal receiver that can house several barrels,

73
00:05:10,441 --> 00:05:14,440
anywhere from a 5.56 millimeter up to a Cal 50,

74
00:05:14,441 --> 00:05:15,440
which is what we have in here right now.

75
00:05:15,441 --> 00:05:17,440
20, 50 grains, Tony.

76
00:05:17,441 --> 00:05:22,440
The firing team includes several munitions experts.

77
00:05:22,441 --> 00:05:25,440
The powder handler passes the charge to the loader,

78
00:05:25,441 --> 00:05:29,440
who is the only one allowed in the range with a live round.

79
00:05:29,441 --> 00:05:33,440
He loads the weapon and locks the blast door.

80
00:05:33,441 --> 00:05:34,440
Ears.

81
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:37,140
Fire.

82
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:48,580
Hardened steel reacts very differently than soft armor.

83
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:53,940
When a bullet hits steel, the rigid steel opposes the bullet's energy.

84
00:05:54,500 --> 00:06:00,760
But an extremely hard bullet will burrow into the surface, expending energy, deforming and flexing the steel.

85
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:03,280
The bullet literally grinds to a halt.

86
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:11,440
This thin tin plate shows how waves of energy radiate through the metal.

87
00:06:11,441 --> 00:06:18,440
Soft bullets can be completely deflected, causing them to ricochet or simply disintegrate against the surface.

88
00:06:18,441 --> 00:06:25,440
Understanding the dynamics of such microstructural events makes it possible to develop an armor that can withstand any bullet.

89
00:06:25,441 --> 00:06:31,440
But it cuts both ways. For every armor, there's a bullet that can pierce it.

90
00:06:31,441 --> 00:06:35,980
For each benefit you find in a new material, it also has its Achilles heel.

91
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:40,840
And history has shown that one of armor's key vulnerabilities is its weight.

92
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:44,980
Really, it's always been a trade-off between protection and mobility,

93
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:50,140
because as you try to increase the protection level to stop bigger and faster bullets,

94
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:51,740
you have to increase weight.

95
00:06:51,741 --> 00:06:53,080
A soldier gets tired.

96
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:57,240
As you increase the weight, it degrades the performance of the soldier on the field.

97
00:06:57,241 --> 00:07:03,540
The conceptual origins of bulletproofing date back to prehistoric times.

98
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:08,680
The thicker the hide, the sharper the spear required to penetrate it.

99
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:13,460
Early swords, spears, and arrows could be defeated by a variety of armors.

100
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:21,840
Leather works on the principle that it doesn't try to deflect a weapon, deflect a blow.

101
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:24,180
It tries to absorb it.

102
00:07:24,181 --> 00:07:27,680
As swords became harder and sharper over the centuries,

103
00:07:27,681 --> 00:07:32,180
inventive armorers developed an array of metal shapes and patterns to defeat them.

104
00:07:32,181 --> 00:07:40,180
Mail, which is interlinked rings of iron, would work very well against a cutting blow.

105
00:07:40,181 --> 00:07:46,180
But mail didn't protect well against a stabbing blow that could part the links.

106
00:07:46,181 --> 00:07:49,180
For that, you needed something more solid.

107
00:07:49,181 --> 00:07:54,140
In the 12th century AD, soldiers began adding pieces of iron plate to their armor.

108
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:59,940
Effective against both blades and projectiles, plate armor became a soldier's best protection.

109
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:07,080
Then in 1360, a terrifying new weapon appeared on the battlefields of Europe.

110
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:13,660
In its earliest form, the gun was no more than a crude handheld cannon,

111
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:18,220
but its potential for penetrating a warrior's armor was obvious.

112
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:27,680
The handgun upped the ante in this competition between the weapon maker and the armorer because

113
00:08:27,681 --> 00:08:33,620
with the chemical power of the bullet, you could get something that hit as hard as the

114
00:08:33,621 --> 00:08:34,820
most powerful crossbow.

115
00:08:34,821 --> 00:08:38,820
It was a very simple primitive weapon that could be issued in large numbers.

116
00:08:38,821 --> 00:08:43,880
By the late 15th century, improvements in the firing mechanism made the gun much more

117
00:08:43,881 --> 00:08:48,240
accurate and popular.

118
00:08:48,241 --> 00:08:52,540
speed bullets were soon able to penetrate iron plating.

119
00:08:52,541 --> 00:08:57,480
It seemed that suits of armor only an eighth of an inch thick would become obsolete.

120
00:08:57,481 --> 00:09:04,420
However, in the 16th century, resourceful armorers countered by adding more carbon to the iron,

121
00:09:04,421 --> 00:09:06,120
creating steel.

122
00:09:06,121 --> 00:09:10,520
Armor that weighed the same as older kinds was now twice as strong.

123
00:09:10,521 --> 00:09:17,840
This was the period when the term bulletproof came into the lexicon.

124
00:09:17,841 --> 00:09:21,200
In the 16th century, to prove was to test.

125
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:25,120
And the armorers would prove their plate armor

126
00:09:25,121 --> 00:09:29,300
by taking a pistol and shooting the pistol into the breastplate.

127
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:30,840
And it would leave a little dent.

128
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:35,260
And whenever someone was buying breastplates,

129
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:36,640
they'd look for that little dent

130
00:09:36,641 --> 00:09:39,300
that proved that breastplate was bulletproof.

131
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:41,900
But by the end of the 16th century,

132
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:44,420
the advantage once again shifted to gun makers,

133
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:47,580
who had designed a new weapon, the musket.

134
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:58,640
It measured almost six feet long, weighed a hefty 20 pounds, and fired a .75 caliber round that could blast through even the heaviest armor.

135
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:02,605
It was the anti-tank weapon of the period.

136
00:10:02,606 --> 00:10:08,400
It upped the anti once again to the point where there was just no question.

137
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:13,160
There was no plate armor around that could stop a musket.

138
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:16,340
Guns were cheap and soldiers plentiful,

139
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:19,640
while armor was expensive and seldom effective.

140
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:24,220
For hundreds of years, the bullet maintained the upper hand

141
00:10:24,221 --> 00:10:26,880
in the race between weapons and protective gear.

142
00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:31,000
By the 19th century, however,

143
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:34,040
those who believed in armor's potential were fighting back.

144
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:38,180
Case in point, Australia's most notorious and innovative outlaw,

145
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:39,340
Ned Kelly.

146
00:10:39,341 --> 00:10:44,660
In 1880, Kelly and his gang constructed crude helmets and body armor.

147
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:50,620
They would learn about their armor's limitations the hard way when these tin can juggernauts faced

148
00:10:50,621 --> 00:10:54,267
off with sheriff's deputies in a Wild West style shootout.

149
00:10:54,268 --> 00:10:56,720
The body armor, which weighed about 100

150
00:10:56,721 --> 00:11:00,961
pounds, slowed them down so much they couldn't escape.

151
00:11:00,962 --> 00:11:04,260
Police eventually shot Kelly's unprotected

152
00:11:04,261 --> 00:11:07,000
legs, and the heavy armor pinned him to the ground.

153
00:11:09,560 --> 00:11:14,100
By World War I, armor mounted a comeback, at least for vehicles,

154
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:15,540
in the form of tanks.

155
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:20,400
Body armor for soldiers didn't become practical again

156
00:11:20,401 --> 00:11:23,460
until World War II, when Army flyers

157
00:11:23,461 --> 00:11:26,920
donned a new form of personal protection, the flak jacket.

158
00:11:27,540 --> 00:11:30,880
The flak jacket consisted of heavy steel plates

159
00:11:30,881 --> 00:11:32,480
suspended in fabric.

160
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:35,480
And this provided very effective protection

161
00:11:35,481 --> 00:11:38,160
for a man who was standing in place.

162
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:42,760
It was not something that could be worn by an infantryman.

163
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:47,560
The infantrymen remained vulnerable until the 1950s,

164
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,120
when experts developed an improved flak jacket

165
00:11:50,121 --> 00:11:51,300
during the Korean War.

166
00:11:52,140 --> 00:11:55,500
Instead of steel, it was lined with rigid plates of aluminum.

167
00:11:56,820 --> 00:11:59,640
By Vietnam, flak jackets had been improved,

168
00:11:59,641 --> 00:12:02,720
but they remained ineffective against the direct hit of a bullet.

169
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:07,220
In the 1960s, the task of rating and improving the jackets

170
00:12:07,221 --> 00:12:10,740
fell to the National Institute of Justice, or NIJ,

171
00:12:11,340 --> 00:12:13,940
and scientists like Dr. David Boyd.

172
00:12:14,620 --> 00:12:19,860
Bullets penetrate vests that aren't penetrable by shrapnel,

173
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:24,080
and so we were interested in trying to find a way to stop pistol bullets.

174
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:27,120
Well, a scientist in NIJ, Lester Shubin,

175
00:12:27,121 --> 00:12:32,540
saw an advertisement for Kevlar, which claimed that this fiber was stronger than steel.

176
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:37,160
Kevlar consisted of tough synthetic fibers in a super-dense weave.

177
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:41,560
Researchers had developed it in the early 1970s to strengthen tires.

178
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:46,700
NIJ scientists decided to use it in some prototype vests.

179
00:12:47,960 --> 00:12:55,740
And so working with the military, we conducted a series of tests to see if they would stop pistol bullets.

180
00:12:55,980 --> 00:12:56,680
They did.

181
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:05,160
At the same time, Detroit businessman Richard Davis, who had been shot by a robber years before, also began experimenting with Kevlar.

182
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:12,260
Davis layered the material in a vest, which he marketed to law enforcement under the name Second Chance.

183
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:18,760
Quite frankly, the officers did not believe that this type of soft material could stop bullets.

184
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:26,640
At that point, he decided to get in front of a large group of police officers in the Detroit area, where he did shoot himself.

185
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:32,120
An 8mm camera captured every moment as Davis put his life on the line.

186
00:13:57,120 --> 00:14:04,120
OK.

187
00:14:04,121 --> 00:14:05,120
It stings.

188
00:14:05,121 --> 00:14:06,120
It stings.

189
00:14:06,121 --> 00:14:07,120
It's enough to make you mad.

190
00:14:07,121 --> 00:14:12,120
I have the skin broken.

191
00:14:12,121 --> 00:14:18,120
The daring all or nothing experiment convinced the officers.

192
00:14:18,121 --> 00:14:25,120
But any cop who's taken a bullet in his armor will tell you the impact is still a shock to the system.

193
00:14:25,121 --> 00:14:29,000
a shock to the system. You might be surprised how much.

194
00:14:32,940 --> 00:14:35,920
A bullet is placed in a gun, ready to fire.

195
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:40,720
Fast-burning gunpowder propels it down the barrel.

196
00:14:41,420 --> 00:14:45,660
The grooved rifling spins the bullet as it accelerates to supersonic speeds.

197
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:48,520
Spinning makes it aerodynamically accurate.

198
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:51,780
Its energy will be transferred to whatever it hits.

199
00:14:52,760 --> 00:14:56,360
If it hits flesh, it will drill through it like a propeller through water.

200
00:14:57,140 --> 00:15:00,920
But if it hits a bulletproof vest, the bullet will be stopped.

201
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:06,540
Still, its energy will continue into the body behind it, causing blunt trauma.

202
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:10,400
Somehow you have to stop all the momentum of the round.

203
00:15:10,401 --> 00:15:14,360
For example, if you're designing a body armor and a bullet comes in and strikes you,

204
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:16,900
all that momentum has to be transferred to you.

205
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:19,160
Even if you stop the round, it's all transferred to you.

206
00:15:19,161 --> 00:15:24,880
The National Institute of Justice, or NIJ, requires all vests to be tested.

207
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:32,500
Not only for stopping power, but also for resistance to blunt trauma.

208
00:15:33,420 --> 00:15:37,640
If it passes, it comes back to us and we issue the certification documents,

209
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:41,420
which goes back to the manufacturer, and we put them in our database

210
00:15:41,421 --> 00:15:43,820
so we can attest that they have in fact passed.

211
00:15:43,821 --> 00:15:50,460
In the testing lab, they use ballistic clay to measure the trauma from a bullet hit.

212
00:15:51,500 --> 00:15:55,180
What you can see here is the different impact from three different rounds.

213
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:59,460
What you're basically seeing in the deeper it goes, usually there's more trauma.

214
00:16:00,060 --> 00:16:05,060
The maximum trauma allowed under the NIJ standard is 44 millimeters.

215
00:16:05,720 --> 00:16:11,400
Blunt trauma is the reason why some people shot while wearing vests suffer bruises or cracked ribs.

216
00:16:11,401 --> 00:16:15,440
Curious to know what it feels like to be shot while wearing armor?

217
00:16:18,760 --> 00:16:23,040
Let's compare an incoming bullet to something more familiar, a baseball.

218
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:28,840
Being hit by a .357 Magnum bullet while wearing soft armor

219
00:16:28,841 --> 00:16:36,040
is like being hit unprotected by a 95-mile-an-hour Major League fastball.

220
00:16:36,041 --> 00:16:42,960
For this reason, soft armor is often combined with hard armor inserts that eliminate blunt

221
00:16:42,961 --> 00:16:43,661
trauma.

222
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:51,340
Beginning in the 1970s, vests included steel inserts, but they were extremely heavy.

223
00:16:54,340 --> 00:16:58,500
Scientists needed something lighter, and they found it in high-tech ceramics.

224
00:17:00,300 --> 00:17:05,340
Ceradyne, based in Southern California, uses advanced technical ceramics to make products

225
00:17:05,341 --> 00:17:07,280
that are the pinnacle of bulletproof materials.

226
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,020
A large portion of the military's ceramic body armor

227
00:17:12,021 --> 00:17:13,420
is manufactured at this plant.

228
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:19,320
The idea of a ceramic stopping a projectile

229
00:17:19,321 --> 00:17:23,000
may be quite alien to the normal way of thinking.

230
00:17:23,540 --> 00:17:27,340
We all know that if we drop a plate, it breaks.

231
00:17:27,341 --> 00:17:31,020
But there are ways of putting a backing on the ceramic

232
00:17:31,021 --> 00:17:33,920
to keep those cracks from propagating.

233
00:17:33,921 --> 00:17:42,920
Ceramics are very hard and as long as they can be kept intact, they're able to completely defeat around without any deformation or failure of the target.

234
00:17:42,921 --> 00:17:51,942
Ceridine produces an extremely dense ceramic made of boron carbide, one of the hardest synthetic substances known.

235
00:17:51,943 --> 00:17:53,920
Only diamonds are harder.

236
00:17:53,921 --> 00:18:00,200
The process initially starts with a powder.

237
00:18:00,201 --> 00:18:06,840
That powder is then formed into a preform in a high pressure press.

238
00:18:06,841 --> 00:18:14,620
We take the preform of the ceramic and then place it into an electrically heated induction

239
00:18:14,621 --> 00:18:16,220
furnace.

240
00:18:16,221 --> 00:18:20,040
The furnace is heated up in excess of 2000 degrees centigrade.

241
00:18:20,041 --> 00:18:22,320
The ceramic is then cleaned.

242
00:18:22,321 --> 00:18:26,020
We use polymers to put the backing on.

243
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:28,680
The polymers are made out of Kevlar,

244
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:30,980
are then bonded with resin

245
00:18:30,981 --> 00:18:36,240
and then compressed onto the back face of the ceramic

246
00:18:36,241 --> 00:18:39,600
in order to ensure that once the projectile

247
00:18:39,601 --> 00:18:41,100
has hit the ceramic and broken,

248
00:18:41,620 --> 00:18:42,800
it doesn't go any further.

249
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:45,540
When a bullet hits ceramic armor,

250
00:18:45,700 --> 00:18:47,040
the armor begins to fracture.

251
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:50,480
Each tiny fracture absorbs more energy.

252
00:18:50,481 --> 00:18:59,240
As the bullet's energy dissipates, the fractures spread wider and wider into a cone that presses against the Kevlar backing.

253
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:04,720
As long as the bullet is pushing against the crushed ceramic, it can't penetrate it.

254
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:13,100
The newest ceramic design is a flexible sheet of armored discs called Dragon Skin, made by Pinnacle Armor in California.

255
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:15,760
The design is so top secret.

256
00:19:16,860 --> 00:19:19,040
This is as much as we're allowed to show you.

257
00:19:19,041 --> 00:19:23,040
Experts prepare to test it at the Aberdeen Testing Center.

258
00:19:23,041 --> 00:19:26,040
This is the next evolution in armor technology

259
00:19:26,041 --> 00:19:29,040
to try to meet some of the more severe direct fire threats.

260
00:19:32,040 --> 00:19:34,040
Go ahead.

261
00:19:34,041 --> 00:19:36,040
Yeah, you guys run down and take a couple of days.

262
00:19:36,041 --> 00:19:37,040
Yes.

263
00:19:43,040 --> 00:19:46,040
As you can see here, this is representative

264
00:19:46,041 --> 00:19:49,040
of what would be the vest portion of the package.

265
00:19:49,041 --> 00:19:53,180
And this is the impact site of the shot we just executed.

266
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:57,100
And obviously there's no residual debris, no signature of any defeat.

267
00:19:57,620 --> 00:19:59,500
The specific results are classified.

268
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:05,940
But Pinnacle Armor has acknowledged that Dragon Skin can stop some of the most powerful military assault rifle bullets.

269
00:20:10,440 --> 00:20:11,740
It's a constant pendulum.

270
00:20:12,160 --> 00:20:16,780
You've got the bullet makers versus the armor guys, and the armor guys generally always lag behind.

271
00:20:16,781 --> 00:20:22,280
But the tide is beginning to turn, and this is one solution that's demonstrated that.

272
00:20:22,720 --> 00:20:25,580
Ceramic armor works so well to reduce blunt trauma,

273
00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:29,520
soldiers sometimes report being hit by bullets without even noticing.

274
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:36,780
Remarkably, materials more brittle than ceramic have been stopping bullets for decades.

275
00:20:39,940 --> 00:20:45,840
Glass, the most fragile of building materials, is so breakable that even a pebble can shatter it.

276
00:20:45,841 --> 00:20:52,540
But when glass is combined with a thin layer of polycarbonate made from plastics, it can stop a bullet effectively.

277
00:20:54,440 --> 00:20:57,200
It essentially becomes a transparent armor.

278
00:20:58,660 --> 00:21:03,980
You need multiple sheets because you don't want the fractures which start on one side to run all the way to the other.

279
00:21:04,400 --> 00:21:12,340
So we have an impact happening and we want to try to localize the damage to the glass so that it actually takes many layers, but it doesn't break all the way through.

280
00:21:12,341 --> 00:21:17,560
Laminated glass was invented in 1909 for use in automobiles.

281
00:21:18,100 --> 00:21:22,800
It consisted of several layers of thick glass with thin, clear plastic in between.

282
00:21:23,520 --> 00:21:25,780
The layers were bonded together by heat.

283
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:29,760
The glass was used in airplane windshields during World War I

284
00:21:29,761 --> 00:21:33,860
and by the 1920s in police vans and armored cars.

285
00:21:34,380 --> 00:21:37,120
But it wasn't just cops who used bulletproof glass.

286
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:40,700
Wealthy criminals paid to have the glass installed in their cars

287
00:21:40,701 --> 00:21:42,920
for protection from the police.

288
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:45,920
But the armor had one flaw.

289
00:21:46,980 --> 00:21:49,880
It was easy to spot because the windshields were flat.

290
00:21:50,800 --> 00:21:53,000
The technology to curve bulletproof glass

291
00:21:53,001 --> 00:21:54,840
wouldn't appear for another decade.

292
00:21:56,340 --> 00:22:00,280
In the 1970s, researchers advanced bulletproof glass

293
00:22:00,281 --> 00:22:01,800
by developing thinner layers

294
00:22:01,801 --> 00:22:04,720
that could flex more before breaking.

295
00:22:05,280 --> 00:22:08,800
That decade, DuPont created SpallShield,

296
00:22:08,801 --> 00:22:14,300
a distinctly improved bulletproof glass for hospitals, storm shelters, and schools.

297
00:22:16,180 --> 00:22:21,620
The glass prevents spalling, the shower of glass that occurs when conventional glass shatters.

298
00:22:22,340 --> 00:22:26,780
It can stop a variety of projectiles from penetrating, from high-powered gunfire

299
00:22:26,781 --> 00:22:35,540
to explosive debris. In the last few decades, bulletproof polycarbonate glass,

300
00:22:35,541 --> 00:22:38,040
created completely from plastics, has made an appearance.

301
00:22:41,080 --> 00:22:44,600
This clear material can crush and absorb a bullet on impact.

302
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:49,520
The polycarbonate is less expensive than real glass with plastic layers,

303
00:22:49,920 --> 00:22:51,920
but it's more difficult to see through.

304
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:56,740
And there's bulletproofing that's even more invisible.

305
00:22:57,820 --> 00:23:00,320
Everywhere we look, though we may not see it,

306
00:23:00,780 --> 00:23:03,160
there's effective protection against gunfire.

307
00:23:05,540 --> 00:23:14,880
When police SWAT teams go into action,

308
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:17,980
their most important piece of gear may be their armor.

309
00:23:19,620 --> 00:23:23,440
At a SWAT training range in northern Illinois known as the Sight,

310
00:23:24,060 --> 00:23:27,460
three SWAT teams from Yorkville, Belvedere, and Freeport

311
00:23:27,461 --> 00:23:30,580
are prepping for joint high-risk training exercises.

312
00:23:30,581 --> 00:23:34,680
All right, the layout is basically the sort of downstairs guys are going to go in.

313
00:23:35,540 --> 00:23:39,820
Their vests are layered with Kevlar and ceramic plates for maximum protection.

314
00:23:40,780 --> 00:23:43,180
What we're doing today is we're simulating a high-risk search warrant.

315
00:23:43,720 --> 00:23:47,040
You'll notice as a team is approaching, they'll approach at multiple points.

316
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:50,440
As they make entry, you'll notice that the team's wearing their hard body armor.

317
00:23:51,100 --> 00:23:53,760
This is a type of environment that is perfect for hard body armor.

318
00:23:55,640 --> 00:23:56,660
Police, search warrant!

319
00:23:57,720 --> 00:23:58,460
Police, stop!

320
00:23:59,960 --> 00:24:03,460
Training exercises like this have helped establish a welcome statistic.

321
00:24:05,540 --> 00:24:14,720
The National Institute of Justice estimates that more than 3,000 officers have been saved from direct hits because of bulletproof vests.

322
00:24:15,620 --> 00:24:18,740
Heavy body armor is what gives us maximum amount of protection.

323
00:24:21,240 --> 00:24:23,200
And safety is always key with us.

324
00:24:23,360 --> 00:24:25,040
Everybody wants to go home at the end of the day.

325
00:24:25,660 --> 00:24:31,180
And having body armor gives you the comfort level to operate in an environment that's hazardous and dangerous.

326
00:24:31,740 --> 00:24:32,840
Could be potentially deadly.

327
00:24:33,640 --> 00:24:35,580
In order to allow free movement,

328
00:24:36,020 --> 00:24:38,000
body armor only covers the vital organs.

329
00:24:38,900 --> 00:24:41,560
It may look like the officer's extremities are very vulnerable.

330
00:24:43,880 --> 00:24:45,680
Let's set up this back room for an ambush.

331
00:24:46,240 --> 00:24:50,000
But the SWAT team's next drill demonstrates that looks can be deceiving.

332
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:53,340
Known as force-on-force training,

333
00:24:53,840 --> 00:24:55,800
it's an intense game of hide-and-seek,

334
00:24:56,160 --> 00:24:57,780
with bad guys lying in ambush,

335
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:00,700
armed with paint-firing simunitions.

336
00:25:00,960 --> 00:25:02,560
It's kind of a paintball gun.

337
00:25:02,840 --> 00:25:04,180
on steroids.

338
00:25:04,181 --> 00:25:05,900
The SWAT team's strategy is designed

339
00:25:05,901 --> 00:25:10,220
to maximize the protective capabilities of their armor.

340
00:25:10,221 --> 00:25:11,780
When we come into a room, what we want to do,

341
00:25:11,781 --> 00:25:13,380
we either want to be moving left or we

342
00:25:13,381 --> 00:25:16,580
want to be moving right as we're identifying our target.

343
00:25:16,581 --> 00:25:17,680
Police, get on the ground!

344
00:25:17,681 --> 00:25:20,460
The quick movement is designed to confuse any armed suspect.

345
00:25:23,100 --> 00:25:24,220
So that way, it's kind of harder

346
00:25:24,221 --> 00:25:26,500
for the person to pick a target up on you.

347
00:25:26,501 --> 00:25:28,540
The reason we wear the body armor is,

348
00:25:28,541 --> 00:25:30,860
if they do pick up a target, this much of our body

349
00:25:30,861 --> 00:25:32,060
is a little safer than normal.

350
00:25:32,840 --> 00:25:39,380
Clear, coming out.

351
00:25:39,640 --> 00:25:40,400
Clear, come out.

352
00:25:45,900 --> 00:25:47,080
Police, get out of the car!

353
00:25:50,380 --> 00:25:51,380
I got a car!

354
00:25:51,640 --> 00:25:55,120
The goggle cam provides a bad guy's view of this rapid assault.

355
00:25:56,780 --> 00:26:00,740
Even when waiting an ambush, suspects have so little time to react,

356
00:26:00,741 --> 00:26:02,960
that choosing targets is nearly impossible.

357
00:26:05,140 --> 00:26:07,660
So they shoot into the officer's center of mass,

358
00:26:08,020 --> 00:26:09,000
right into the vest.

359
00:26:09,740 --> 00:26:11,660
What happened when the officer first walked in,

360
00:26:12,060 --> 00:26:13,520
you could see he got shot in the chest.

361
00:26:13,960 --> 00:26:15,340
If this was a real-life incident,

362
00:26:15,480 --> 00:26:17,620
this officer would have been saved by the body armor

363
00:26:17,621 --> 00:26:19,680
and not suffered any kind of damage at all.

364
00:26:21,760 --> 00:26:24,620
The tactical advantages of armor are so evident

365
00:26:24,621 --> 00:26:28,480
that even the bad guys have started using it.

366
00:26:28,481 --> 00:26:32,080
February 28, 1997.

367
00:26:32,081 --> 00:26:34,880
In a scene reminiscent of the Ned Kelly gang,

368
00:26:34,881 --> 00:26:38,320
two bank robbers shielded from head to toe by body armor

369
00:26:38,321 --> 00:26:40,400
stormed a Bank of America branch

370
00:26:40,401 --> 00:26:41,760
in North Hollywood, California.

371
00:26:41,761 --> 00:26:44,320
As police surrounded the building,

372
00:26:44,321 --> 00:26:48,000
the robbers calmly walked out, machine guns blasting.

373
00:26:48,001 --> 00:26:51,760
The handguns used by police

374
00:26:51,761 --> 00:26:53,280
couldn't penetrate their body armor,

375
00:26:53,281 --> 00:26:55,840
so the criminals casually escaped from the bank.

376
00:26:55,841 --> 00:27:03,820
But patrol officers did not have any weapon in the field that was able to penetrate that vest.

377
00:27:04,900 --> 00:27:10,420
Police eventually stopped them by grabbing higher-powered weapons and shooting the armor-clad robbers,

378
00:27:10,820 --> 00:27:13,440
one in the head and the other in the legs.

379
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:15,980
Both suspects died.

380
00:27:16,820 --> 00:27:19,100
The policemen were outgunned by the suspects.

381
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:21,700
They've now got better weapons

382
00:27:21,701 --> 00:27:26,060
so that they can match or exceed the capability of the suspects.

383
00:27:26,600 --> 00:27:28,920
LAPD went to a larger caliber handgun,

384
00:27:29,240 --> 00:27:34,360
and also now they have AR-15s readily available to the patrolman

385
00:27:34,361 --> 00:27:38,080
so he can defeat the body armor that the suspects were wearing.

386
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:42,040
The shootout not only compelled police to rearm,

387
00:27:42,540 --> 00:27:44,720
but also architects to armor buildings.

388
00:27:46,340 --> 00:27:48,940
Over 1,100 rounds were fired that day,

389
00:27:49,100 --> 00:27:56,100
The bank and surrounding businesses were riddled with bullet holes that penetrated the walls.

390
00:27:56,101 --> 00:28:03,100
Armor Techs, a division of Safeguard Security in Texas, fortifies walls and doors with a

391
00:28:03,101 --> 00:28:06,100
lightweight bullet-resistant barrier made of fiberglass.

392
00:28:06,101 --> 00:28:12,100
Bullet-resistant fiberglass was introduced into the industry because it needed a new option for bullet-resistant protection.

393
00:28:12,101 --> 00:28:14,100
It's very light and it's easy to install.

394
00:28:14,101 --> 00:28:16,100
This is where the bullet actually penetrates here.

395
00:28:16,101 --> 00:28:20,980
and the panel is designed so that the resin and the glass will separate at impact and as it does,

396
00:28:20,981 --> 00:28:26,780
it absorbs the projectile here. Safeguard Security houses the complete manufacturing

397
00:28:26,781 --> 00:28:29,024
line for these super tough panels.

398
00:28:29,025 --> 00:28:32,720
Textile looms weave together the ballistic grade fibers.

399
00:28:32,721 --> 00:28:38,220
We start with the fabrics in our own textile operation and we take a special woven roving

400
00:28:38,221 --> 00:28:39,450
that we've designed.

401
00:28:39,451 --> 00:28:43,940
It's got a special amount of yarns per inch of material that allow us to,

402
00:28:43,941 --> 00:28:48,300
in conjunction with bullet-resistant resins that we use to make a flat, rigid panel.

403
00:28:49,160 --> 00:28:52,800
When finished, the panels are sold separately to reinforce walls.

404
00:28:53,420 --> 00:28:56,740
They're also installed in metal doors manufactured on site.

405
00:28:58,160 --> 00:29:02,460
What we do there is we take a metal skin, we put the fiberglass in the core of the door,

406
00:29:02,880 --> 00:29:06,660
and we put another skin over that, and we weld it closed and make a hollow metal door.

407
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:13,040
The company's experts also fortify wooden doors.

408
00:29:13,940 --> 00:29:18,820
We take our bullet-resistant fiberglass panel and we adhere wood on both sides.

409
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:21,220
Then we take a wood veneer and adhere it to that.

410
00:29:21,780 --> 00:29:27,920
Then we take that compound and we put it into a press to make sure it's securely fastened to the door.

411
00:29:28,280 --> 00:29:31,380
Then we take it out of the press and we install it on the frame.

412
00:29:34,320 --> 00:29:41,400
Without calling attention to themselves, walls and doors like these add that extra measure of security to homes and offices.

413
00:29:42,300 --> 00:29:44,600
But what can you do when you hit the road?

414
00:29:49,540 --> 00:29:53,020
6.30 a.m., the White House hits the road.

415
00:29:55,420 --> 00:29:59,320
Wherever an American president travels, on land, sea, or air,

416
00:29:59,820 --> 00:30:01,800
he's surrounded by a bubble of protection

417
00:30:01,801 --> 00:30:04,320
made up of skilled Secret Service agents

418
00:30:04,321 --> 00:30:06,320
and bulletproofing technology.

419
00:30:07,500 --> 00:30:10,880
The presidential limousine is the most bulletproofed car in history.

420
00:30:11,400 --> 00:30:14,100
And the rest of the cars in the motorcade may look ordinary,

421
00:30:14,700 --> 00:30:16,680
but they're a heavily armored rolling arsenal.

422
00:30:18,800 --> 00:30:24,320
This car is a 1984 Cadillac presidential limousine development prototype.

423
00:30:24,920 --> 00:30:28,720
This car was built by General Motors so they can evaluate the overall layout

424
00:30:28,721 --> 00:30:32,640
of the presidential vehicle that they were building for then-president Ronald Reagan.

425
00:30:32,641 --> 00:30:37,980
Virtually everything on this car is armor-plated or specially strengthened

426
00:30:37,981 --> 00:30:41,600
to withstand the rigors of a chase or gunfire.

427
00:30:42,100 --> 00:30:44,360
It's got behind what appears to be a standard grille

428
00:30:44,361 --> 00:30:46,880
is actually something that deflects the bullets

429
00:30:46,881 --> 00:30:48,580
to prevent them from going into the radiator,

430
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:51,340
because if your car overheats, you're not going to get very far.

431
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:55,420
Moving back, what you'll notice is something

432
00:30:55,421 --> 00:30:59,420
that I think just about everybody's familiar with or heard of.

433
00:30:59,820 --> 00:31:03,960
These are run-flat tires, and they'll withstand numerous bullet hits.

434
00:31:05,020 --> 00:31:07,860
A run-flat tire is like a tire within a tire.

435
00:31:07,980 --> 00:31:12,980
It's air cushioned on the outside with a special solid rubber core.

436
00:31:12,981 --> 00:31:17,980
If a bullet punctures the outer skin, the air cushion deflates,

437
00:31:17,981 --> 00:31:20,980
but only a short distance from the rubber core,

438
00:31:20,981 --> 00:31:23,980
allowing the car to maneuver and escape at high speeds.

439
00:31:23,981 --> 00:31:26,980
Moving farther back, we get to the passenger compartment,

440
00:31:26,981 --> 00:31:29,980
and this is probably the most important of all.

441
00:31:29,981 --> 00:31:35,980
We have a windshield that is protected by a layer of special material

442
00:31:35,981 --> 00:31:38,120
that's very highly bullet resistant.

443
00:31:38,620 --> 00:31:42,240
And as we move back, we have the pillars that are even bullet resistant.

444
00:31:42,880 --> 00:31:48,140
And you open the door, and in addition to the bulletproof glass,

445
00:31:48,480 --> 00:31:51,320
you'll notice that there's a special lip around the inside

446
00:31:51,321 --> 00:31:54,080
that even if a bullet did manage to get through the gap,

447
00:31:54,420 --> 00:31:56,180
it wouldn't enter the passenger compartment.

448
00:31:56,580 --> 00:32:00,560
There's a lot of headroom, and when the door is open,

449
00:32:00,820 --> 00:32:03,720
people will glimpse this thickness here,

450
00:32:03,721 --> 00:32:11,780
And they'll notice that it's really no different than a regular car, than a regular Cadillac of the day, because all the armoring is inside.

451
00:32:12,500 --> 00:32:18,880
The car was built by O'Gara Hess and Eisenhardt, the world's largest passenger vehicle armoring company.

452
00:32:19,920 --> 00:32:24,480
It's fortified limousines for every president since Harry S. Truman in 1948.

453
00:32:28,540 --> 00:32:31,820
But presidents aren't the only ones looking for protection on the road.

454
00:32:32,560 --> 00:32:36,580
Texas Armoring of San Antonio armors everyday passenger vehicles.

455
00:32:37,340 --> 00:32:42,060
Most of the clients in the United States have been political figures or religious leaders.

456
00:32:42,720 --> 00:32:49,780
Texas Armoring relies on SpectraShield, a super light ballistics material to bulletproof their vehicles and lighten the load.

457
00:32:51,040 --> 00:32:58,060
Similar to Kevlar, SpectraShield has multiple layers and is pressed with a resin to form a hard bullet-resistant panel.

458
00:32:58,061 --> 00:33:03,200
To armor the door, we would take off the interior door panel

459
00:33:03,201 --> 00:33:06,700
and then put spectra shield into the door.

460
00:33:07,540 --> 00:33:10,660
And then when there's not a space where the spectra shield can fit

461
00:33:10,661 --> 00:33:12,320
because of the thickness of the spectra shield,

462
00:33:12,580 --> 00:33:14,320
we would replace it with ballistic steel.

463
00:33:14,600 --> 00:33:16,700
Then we would replace the door panel,

464
00:33:16,800 --> 00:33:18,740
and all the wiring basically stays the same.

465
00:33:18,980 --> 00:33:21,800
Then the window would be placed in and it would be fixed.

466
00:33:22,620 --> 00:33:25,920
The process is repeated throughout the car with various materials.

467
00:33:28,060 --> 00:33:35,180
When we armor a vehicle, the entire passenger compartment, which is the whole cabin area of the vehicle, will be protected,

468
00:33:35,560 --> 00:33:45,160
as well as the gas tank, the battery, the radiator, which would allow a vehicle more time to get out of the situation.

469
00:33:45,880 --> 00:33:49,560
The floor and ceiling of the cab are lined with thick ballistic nylon.

470
00:33:49,561 --> 00:33:57,660
The high hardened steel is the material we use in the firewall and in the pillars and

471
00:33:57,661 --> 00:34:06,240
posts everywhere that needs to be protected against direct hits of high power rifle.

472
00:34:06,241 --> 00:34:13,100
Like Texas Armoring, O'Gara, Hess, and Eisenhardt armor many luxury cars for the private sector.

473
00:34:13,101 --> 00:34:15,840
And its employees stand by their product.

474
00:34:15,841 --> 00:34:20,320
For a promotional film, regional marketing director Michael Sellers volunteered to take

475
00:34:20,321 --> 00:34:22,840
the wheel in a test using real ammunition.

476
00:34:22,841 --> 00:34:24,840
Fire and hold.

477
00:34:24,841 --> 00:34:25,840
Action.

478
00:34:25,841 --> 00:34:26,840
Okay.

479
00:34:26,841 --> 00:34:30,840
Not bad.

480
00:34:30,841 --> 00:34:44,840
Still here, folks.

481
00:34:45,840 --> 00:34:49,720
O'Gara Hess and Eisenhardt also armor vehicles for the military.

482
00:34:50,580 --> 00:34:56,241
We are the sole source provider of the up-armored Humvee for the U.S.

483
00:34:56,242 --> 00:34:57,800
Army and Air Force.

484
00:34:58,480 --> 00:35:05,400
And this is a wheeled armored vehicle that's capable of defeating assault rifle threats and landmine attacks.

485
00:35:06,260 --> 00:35:12,440
In the business of bulletproofing, preventing enemies from knowing what's armored can give defenders a distinct advantage.

486
00:35:12,441 --> 00:35:18,440
But sometimes the idea is to look as impenetrable as possible.

487
00:35:21,240 --> 00:35:27,420
SWAT teams own highly fortified, intimidating vehicles they humbly call personnel carriers.

488
00:35:28,800 --> 00:35:35,060
In the old days, we might have to run up with a police car and position that in between us so we could rescue the victim.

489
00:35:35,260 --> 00:35:38,940
The guys that were driving that car or the policemen that were doing that rescue were in great danger.

490
00:35:38,941 --> 00:35:44,080
Now at least we have the capability of coming up with a ballistic vehicle

491
00:35:44,081 --> 00:35:47,380
that'll stop a lot of the rounds that the suspects are shooting.

492
00:35:49,440 --> 00:35:51,980
Inside, it's got very small windshields

493
00:35:51,981 --> 00:35:54,360
because they want the majority of the vehicle to be steel.

494
00:35:54,780 --> 00:35:57,960
It actually has a shield that pops up and protects somebody

495
00:35:57,961 --> 00:36:00,620
who can be up inside that hole with a gun,

496
00:36:00,740 --> 00:36:03,020
providing cover fire or whatever we might need them to do.

497
00:36:03,360 --> 00:36:06,460
It's a very heavy vehicle. It weighs about 10,000 pounds.

498
00:36:06,461 --> 00:36:10,460
The back of the vehicle has two bench seats on either side,

499
00:36:10,461 --> 00:36:15,460
which can accommodate approximately six uniformed SWAT officers in the back.

500
00:36:15,461 --> 00:36:20,460
Humans aren't the only cargo transported by these traveling strongholds.

501
00:36:20,461 --> 00:36:23,460
Money is by far the largest haul.

502
00:36:23,461 --> 00:36:26,460
Loomis, one of America's biggest money haulers,

503
00:36:26,461 --> 00:36:29,460
transports massive amounts of currency for the U.S. government.

504
00:36:29,461 --> 00:36:35,460
The imposing Loomis trucks, which weigh over 60,000 pounds,

505
00:36:35,461 --> 00:36:38,800
are armored throughout with steel and aluminum plating

506
00:36:38,801 --> 00:36:41,600
that can withstand multiple high-powered gunshots.

507
00:36:42,660 --> 00:36:45,180
Remarkably, these rolling vaults are handmade.

508
00:36:46,840 --> 00:36:49,900
To build an armored truck, we order in the material,

509
00:36:50,180 --> 00:36:51,760
which is usually aluminum plate.

510
00:36:51,920 --> 00:36:54,480
We bring it together, cut it to size,

511
00:36:54,920 --> 00:36:56,980
and then we lay it out on what we call a jig,

512
00:36:57,460 --> 00:37:00,860
and we fully weld the armored truck inside and out.

513
00:37:01,580 --> 00:37:03,840
Surprisingly, the walls used to create the frame

514
00:37:03,841 --> 00:37:09,680
are made not of heavy steel, but of lightweight aluminum armor.

515
00:37:09,681 --> 00:37:12,620
Heavily fortified aluminum can absorb the blow of a bullet

516
00:37:12,621 --> 00:37:16,260
by deforming, but not splitting.

517
00:37:16,261 --> 00:37:18,780
Once the frame is built, it's mounted on the chassis

518
00:37:18,781 --> 00:37:20,240
and painted.

519
00:37:20,241 --> 00:37:23,120
Now it's ready to protect a mother load of cold, hard cash.

520
00:37:25,980 --> 00:37:28,420
Some clients require an armored car that allows them

521
00:37:28,421 --> 00:37:31,480
to shoot back at an attacker.

522
00:37:31,481 --> 00:37:40,100
IBISTECH in Pennsylvania has created a unique bulletproof vehicle with a remote-controlled, large-caliber machine gun that can do just that.

523
00:37:40,600 --> 00:37:46,500
The company produces about 100 trucks a year, with asking prices of up to $500,000.

524
00:37:47,360 --> 00:37:56,220
IBISTECH has developed the Cobra Viper in response to known threats in particularly the Middle East.

525
00:37:56,960 --> 00:37:59,540
Our objective was to provide security and protection,

526
00:37:59,760 --> 00:38:01,740
which does not look offensive in nature.

527
00:38:02,280 --> 00:38:04,940
The gun is operated from the passenger side of the vehicle.

528
00:38:06,120 --> 00:38:07,540
It is fully stabilized.

529
00:38:07,860 --> 00:38:12,300
It has gyroscopes that determine the movement of the vehicle,

530
00:38:12,420 --> 00:38:14,380
the movement of the weapon as it's being fired,

531
00:38:14,860 --> 00:38:16,720
so that it stabilizes the system,

532
00:38:16,940 --> 00:38:19,940
so that you get very accurate fire, even on the move.

533
00:38:20,280 --> 00:38:23,160
The high-caliber machine gun can strike a target.

534
00:38:23,161 --> 00:38:26,760
Piper, commit to fire in three, two, one.

535
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:31,100
More than 1,000 yards away.

536
00:38:33,200 --> 00:38:36,240
As bulletproofing technology continues to advance,

537
00:38:36,900 --> 00:38:39,040
armor is becoming not only stronger and lighter,

538
00:38:39,420 --> 00:38:40,200
but more flexible.

539
00:38:41,460 --> 00:38:45,480
What if bulletproof armor could be made as flexible as liquid?

540
00:38:53,160 --> 00:39:00,040
In the line of duty, police officers and soldiers are called upon to take dramatic action.

541
00:39:02,680 --> 00:39:06,400
Functioning effectively in armor requires constant training and practice.

542
00:39:10,760 --> 00:39:15,940
Officers like Commander John Caduto must balance protection against mobility.

543
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:23,060
From a standing position behind the cover, a heavier Kevlar, which is what I'm wearing here,

544
00:39:23,160 --> 00:39:25,660
is pretty comfortable, it's not too bad.

545
00:39:25,661 --> 00:39:27,940
As we move to different positions though,

546
00:39:27,941 --> 00:39:30,700
and we get into positions such as this rollover prone,

547
00:39:32,020 --> 00:39:34,020
staying behind cover with my rifle

548
00:39:34,021 --> 00:39:36,260
properly mounted on my shoulder,

549
00:39:36,261 --> 00:39:37,780
gets a little bit more difficult.

550
00:39:38,700 --> 00:39:40,920
The bulkier you make the vest,

551
00:39:40,921 --> 00:39:42,360
the more limited your mobility is,

552
00:39:42,361 --> 00:39:44,980
the harder it is to get into certain tactical positions.

553
00:39:44,981 --> 00:39:48,620
The softer Kevlar is much easier, much thinner,

554
00:39:48,621 --> 00:39:51,320
much more pliable, you have much more mobility,

555
00:39:51,321 --> 00:39:52,920
but yet it doesn't afford you the protection

556
00:39:52,921 --> 00:39:55,320
like the heavier Kavala does, so it's a trade-off.

557
00:39:57,320 --> 00:39:59,400
He could choose to be completely armored,

558
00:39:59,840 --> 00:40:02,560
but then he'd lumber along like this bomb squad officer.

559
00:40:04,360 --> 00:40:06,720
A new type of armor may offer a solution.

560
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:10,860
Meet the soldier of tomorrow.

561
00:40:12,060 --> 00:40:14,500
Technologically enhanced and armored from head to toe.

562
00:40:14,940 --> 00:40:17,380
At the center of this high-tech combat system

563
00:40:17,381 --> 00:40:21,240
is a bulletproof suit composed of liquid armor.

564
00:40:22,920 --> 00:40:26,620
It's now in development at MIT's Hatsopolis Microfluids Lab,

565
00:40:27,460 --> 00:40:29,660
under the direction of Dr. Gareth McKinley.

566
00:40:30,560 --> 00:40:34,020
So there's a lot of work going on in shear thickening fluids.

567
00:40:34,160 --> 00:40:37,380
That's fluids that spontaneously change their properties

568
00:40:37,381 --> 00:40:40,640
when you just hit them with a hammer or with a large impact.

569
00:40:43,520 --> 00:40:46,820
Sheer thickening fluid composed of polyethylene glycol

570
00:40:46,821 --> 00:40:50,440
and tiny nanoparticles has an unusual characteristic.

571
00:40:50,441 --> 00:40:59,580
When a bullet impacts the armor, the intense pressure squeezes the particles, which instantly form a stiff matrix.

572
00:40:59,960 --> 00:41:03,580
The stronger the force of the bullet, the harder the particles lock together.

573
00:41:04,580 --> 00:41:08,940
After the bullet's energy dissipates, the armor becomes soft and flexible again.

574
00:41:10,740 --> 00:41:15,900
The protective properties can even be increased by making the nanoparticles magnetic.

575
00:41:15,901 --> 00:41:20,660
What we're working with is a class of fluids called magnetorheological fluids.

576
00:41:21,220 --> 00:41:25,460
These are liquids that change their properties when we apply a magnetic field to them.

577
00:41:25,900 --> 00:41:29,060
The liquid turns into something that more resembles a solid.

578
00:41:31,320 --> 00:41:34,540
As experts continue to advance bulletproofing technology,

579
00:41:35,000 --> 00:41:38,520
fashion designers are pioneering new trends in bullet-resistant attire.

580
00:41:39,220 --> 00:41:43,020
What was once a luxury item you'd find hanging only in James Bond's closet

581
00:41:43,021 --> 00:41:45,700
is now available to all of us.

582
00:41:45,900 --> 00:41:54,400
Miguel Caballero Limited of Colombia likes to bill itself as the Armani of Bulletproof

583
00:41:54,401 --> 00:41:55,860
clothing.

584
00:41:55,861 --> 00:42:02,780
Its tailored sport coats and leather jackets for all occasions conceal layers of Kevlar.

585
00:42:02,781 --> 00:42:09,300
And at Bulletproof Me, windbreakers and coats are best sellers, along with dinner jackets

586
00:42:09,301 --> 00:42:11,680
and raincoats.

587
00:42:11,681 --> 00:42:17,320
As the fashionable find new ways to protect themselves from bullets flying in the streets,

588
00:42:17,321 --> 00:42:22,600
NASA is using Kevlar bulletproofing to protect the International Space Station from debris

589
00:42:22,601 --> 00:42:23,600
orbiting Earth.

590
00:42:23,601 --> 00:42:27,820
There are two areas where you're concerned.

591
00:42:27,821 --> 00:42:31,460
One is from micrometeoroid or meteoroid impacts on the spacecraft.

592
00:42:31,461 --> 00:42:36,360
The other one is actually from orbital debris, pieces of stuff we've left up there on previous

593
00:42:36,361 --> 00:42:37,360
missions.

594
00:42:37,361 --> 00:42:38,960
And there's a lot of it up there now.

595
00:42:38,961 --> 00:42:42,300
With such mind-boggling possibilities on the horizon,

596
00:42:42,301 --> 00:42:45,460
bulletproofing technology may one day lead to a world

597
00:42:45,461 --> 00:42:50,400
where everyone and everything is routinely armored.

598
00:42:50,401 --> 00:42:53,600
The clothes we wear, the cars we drive,

599
00:42:53,601 --> 00:42:56,580
and the buildings we inhabit.

600
00:42:56,581 --> 00:42:59,340
But if history is our guide, new weapons

601
00:42:59,341 --> 00:43:02,320
will continually emerge, compelling bulletproofing

602
00:43:02,321 --> 00:43:04,460
technology to keep pace.

